  "name": "INHIBIN - B",
  "code": "2786",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Note</strong></span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">1. All result interpretation should be in conjunction with clinical evaluation of the patient and other diagnostic procedures.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">2. Elevation of Inhibin B after treatment is suggestive of residual, recurrent or progressive disease.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">3. Inhibin values fluctuate during the menstrual cycle thus levels in premenopausal women should be interpreted with caution</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Comments</strong></span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Inhibins are protein hormones secreted by Granulosa cells of the ovary in females and Sertoli cells of the testes in males. Inhibin B is </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">primarily produced by small developing follicles and fluctuates during the menstrual cycle. It increases early in the follicular phase to m</span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">reach a peak corresponding with mid-follicular phase decline in FSH levels and decreases in late follicular phase. Inhibin B levels are </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">elevated in 89-100% patients with Granulosa cell tumors and 55-60% patients with Epithelial ovarian tumors. Normal Inhibin B level does&nbsp; </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">not rule out a Mucinous or Granulosa cell ovarian tumor.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Clinical Use</strong></span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">1. As an aid in the diagnosis &amp; monitoring of Granulosa cell tumors and Mucinous epithelial ovarian tumors</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">2. To assess ovarian reserve, inhibin B level in the postmenopausal range is suggestive of diminished or depleted ovarian reserve.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">3. As an adjunct to FSH testing during Infertility evaluation.</span></p>",
